 "notes": [
  "ORDER MATTERS: first match wins. The seleucid-empire polygon is a generous default (everything not claimed earlier, between lat 35.3 and 40.6 and lon 25.6-46); it includes sea. Assign only Anatolian provinces with it, never European ones, and treat sea cells as not land.",
  "ptolemaic-egypt has its capital Alexandria off the map (capital.offMap=true): the polity holds only exclaves in Anatolia. The lead must decide whether to write a polity with an off-map capital or to leave capitalSettlementId null.",
  "No Seleucid or Ptolemaic polity exists yet in the repo (only ptolemaic-cyrenaica appears, in one test, and cyrene). Both are new ids.",
  "Cohesion baseline quirk: macedon, epirus, athens etc. are not in COHESION_BY_POLITY, so they run at DEFAULT_COHESION 3000 even as monarchies. The values here follow the intended scale in the comments (state 7000+, kingdom slightly lower, confederation about 4000, peoples 2200-3000), not that accident.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Cilicia Pedias (Tarsus) is drawn Seleucid; Ptolemy II may have held more of Cilicia after the First Syrian War (274-271). Cilicia Tracheia's eastern end (Soloi) is drawn Ptolemaic only to lon 34.35.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Miletus, Ephesus and Samos were Ptolemaic or Seleucid at different moments between 280 and 260; they stay in the existing ionia-communities polity (autonomous cities) rather than in either empire.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Cius and the Bosporus east shore: Bithynian or independent (Chalcedon is independent; Cius later belongs to Bithynia). Cius is drawn Bithynian.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Amastris and Tium are drawn under Heraclea Pontica; they were briefly its subjects in the 280s.",
  "UNCERTAIN: the Galatian tribal territories are Strabo's, describing c. 100 BCE-AD 20. In 270 the Galatians had been in Anatolia for only 7-8 years and still moved about; the polygons are their eventual homelands. Pessinus was a Phrygian temple state, probably not yet Galatian.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Pontus in 270 is only the interior around Amaseia and Zela; Sinope, Amisos and Trapezus are independent Greek cities, and Pontus took Sinope only in 183 BCE.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Cappadocian Melitene/Cataonia/Tyanitis are drawn Cappadocian; Seleucid nominal claims there are ignored. The Cappadocian kings' dates around 270 are the weakest of any ruler here.",
  "UNCERTAIN: Armenia's polygon runs to the Iranian and Georgian borders and folds in Sophene and Van; Commagene and Osrhoene (Samosata, Edessa) are left with the Seleucid default. Tao and Ardahan may be Iberian rather than Armenian.",
  "Not covered: Cyprus (out of scope), European Thrace and the Chersonese (existing thracian polities), Georgian Iberia (outside Turkey), the Cyclades (existing cycladic-islanders). Cos is drawn Ptolemaic.",
  "Settlement sizes and fortification levels use the scale of punic-wars-scenario.ts (size 12-100, fortification 1-5), chosen by rank, not sourced. Coordinates are from memory; only Pessinus was checked against a source. Tavium (Buyuknefes) is +-0.1 deg."
 ]
}